      <div class="prose-body">

        <h2>AI is already part of learning English</h2>

        <p>Grammar checkers, chatbots you can practise conversations with, apps that correct your pronunciation: all of it is available at any hour, and a lot of it is free. So it's a fair question whether learners still need a teacher.</p>

        <p>I think they do, and the reasons have less to do with technology than with how people actually learn to speak a language.</p>

        <h2>What AI does well</h2>

        <p>Quite a lot. It can produce example sentences, drill vocabulary, explain a grammar point for the third time without losing patience, and correct a paragraph in seconds. For learners who are shy about speaking in front of other people, practising with a chatbot first can take some of the fear out of it.</p>

        <h2>Where it falls short</h2>

        <p>Speaking is a social skill. When a student goes quiet, a teacher can usually tell whether they've forgotten a word or lost their nerve, and deals with each differently. A good class gives people somewhere safe to get things wrong, and that's where most of the learning happens. A chatbot will keep the conversation going, but it notices very little about the person on the other end.</p>

        <p>There's a fairness problem too. The British Council has written about how AI detection tools can flag writing by second-language speakers as machine-generated, partly because it doesn't read like a native speaker's. For a learner, that means honest work can be treated as cheating.</p>

        <h2>Using AI sensibly</h2>

        <p>None of this means avoiding AI. Used well, it can take care of the repetitive practice and leave more class time for the things a teacher is there for: conversation, feedback, and helping someone past the point where they freeze.</p>

        <h2>How we use it</h2>

        <p>In our lessons, speaking comes first. Students are welcome to use whatever tools help them between classes, but the progress comes from talking: in lessons, with each other, and on the Dublin programme, with students from all over Europe.</p>

